1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,631
What is the difference between ionic and covalent bonding? The answer is what happens to the electrons.

2
00:00:06,581 --> 00:00:10,819
Sodium is a metal with one outer electron. Chlorine is a non-metal with seven.

3
00:00:11,168 --> 00:00:15,476
In ionic bonding, sodium transfers its outer electron to chlorine.

4
00:00:15,826 --> 00:00:21,967
Sodium becomes a positive ion, and chlorine becomes a negative ion. Both now have full outer shells.

5
00:00:22,317 --> 00:00:27,821
Opposite charges attract. That electrostatic attraction between the ions is the ionic bond.

6
00:00:28,771 --> 00:00:34,030
Now compare two chlorine atoms. Each needs one more electron, and neither will give one up.

7
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:37,445
So instead of a transfer, their outer shells overlap.

8
00:00:37,795 --> 00:00:40,070
They share a pair of electrons between them.

9
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:45,401
Each chlorine atom now counts eight outer electrons. That shared pair is a covalent bond.

10
00:00:46,351 --> 00:00:47,965
Let's put them side by side.

11
00:00:48,315 --> 00:00:53,400
In ionic bonds, electrons are transferred. In covalent bonds, they are shared.

12
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:59,555
Ionic bonds usually form between a metal and a non-metal. Covalent bonds form between non-metals.

13
00:00:59,905 --> 00:01:06,070
Ionic compounds are made of charged ions held by attraction. Covalent compounds are made of molecules.

14
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:10,321
Table salt is ionic, while water and chlorine gas are covalent.

15
00:01:11,271 --> 00:01:17,761
To recap: in an ionic bond, electrons transfer from a metal to a non-metal, and the ions attract.

16
00:01:18,111 --> 00:01:22,406
In a covalent bond, non-metal atoms share pairs of electrons.

17
00:01:22,756 --> 00:01:27,435
Either way, the atoms end up with full outer shells, which makes them more stable.
